L000085	\keywords{phase oscillators, Kuramoto-Sakaguchi model, finite-size fluctuations, mean-field approach}
L000086	
L000087	\begin{abstract}
L000088	We develop an ab initio approach to describe the statistical behavior of finite-size fluctuations in the deterministic Kuramoto-Sakaguchi model. We obtain explicit expressions for the covariance function of fluctuations of the complex order parameter and determine the variance of its magnitude entirely in terms of the equation parameters. Our results rely on an explicit complex-valued formula for the solution of the Adler equation. We present analytical results for both the sub- and the super-critical case. Moreover, our framework does not require any prior knowledge about the structure of the partially synchronized state. We corroborate our results with numerical simulations of the full Kuramoto-Sakaguchi model. The proposed methodology is sufficiently general such that it can be applied to other interacting particle systems.
L000089	\end{abstract}
L000090	
L000091	
L000092	\maketitle
L000093	
L000094	
L000095	{\it Introduction.}
L000096	Synchronization is a generic phenomenon inherent in interacting oscillators
L000097	that determines their ability to exhibit a wide range of complex dynamical behavior
L000098	\cite{Kuramoto84, Str2000,PikovskyRosenblumKurths01, AceBVRS2005, ArenasEtAl08, DorflerBullo14, RodriguesEtAl16}.
L000099	It has been observed in a plethora of natural and engineered  systems,
L000100	including pace-maker cells of circadian rhythms \cite{YamaguchiETAl03},
L000101	networks of neurons \cite{BhowmikShanahan12}, chemical oscillators \cite{KissEtAl02,TaylorEtAl09}
L000102	and power grids \cite{FilatrellaEtAl08}.
L000103	The main features of synchronization are captured
L000104	by the paradigmatic Kuramoto-Sakaguchi (KS) model~\cite{SakK1986}
L000105	\begin{equation}
L000106	\dot{\psi}_i = \omega_i + \fr{K}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N \sin( \psi_j - \psi_i - \lambda )
L000107	\label{Eq:KS:original}
L000108	\end{equation}
L000109	for the collective behavior of a system of $N$ all-to-all coupled phase oscillators.
L000110	In this model, $\omega_i$ are natural frequencies drawn randomly and independently
L000111	from a given distribution $g(\omega)$ and $K$ and $\lambda$ are real parameters
L000112	quantifying the coupling strength and the phase lag, respectively.
L000113	It is often convenient to rewrite Eq.~\eqref{Eq:KS:original} as
L000114	$$
L000115	\dot{\psi_i} = \omega_i + K\: \mathrm{Im}\left( Z(t) e^{-i \psi_i} e^{-i \lambda} \right),
L000116	$$
L000117	which reveals that each oscillator interacts
L000118	with the other oscillators only via the mean field
L000119	\begin{equation}
L000120	Z(t) = \fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N e^{i \psi_j(t)},
L000121	\label{Def:Z}
L000122	\end{equation}
L000123	which is also coined the complex order parameter and quantifies the degree of synchronization.
L000124	
L000125	A fully synchronized state with $\theta_1 = \theta_2 = \dots = \theta_N$ is characterized by $r(t)=|Z(t)|=1$,
L000126	whereas a completely disordered (incoherent) state is characterized
L000127	by $r(t) = \mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{N})$.
L000128	It is well known that for sufficiently large values of the coupling strength $K$ the system becomes fully synchronized, whereas for sufficiently small values of $K$ the oscillators behave independently and form an incoherent state. For intermediate values of the coupling strength, partially synchronized states are observed in which $r(t)$ fluctuates around a constant value between $0$ and $1$. On a microscopic level the oscillator population splits into two groups: coherent oscillators $\mathcal{C}$, which are phase locked, and rogue oscillators $\mathcal{R}$, which drift with respect to the mean field.
L000129	
L000130	In the thermodynamic limit of infinitely many oscillators each oscillator interacts with the other oscillators via a constant collective mean. This limit is well understood and described by mean-field theory~\cite{SakK1986,OmeW2012,OmeW2013,OttA2008}. In particular, the rotation frequency of the coherent oscillators $\Omega_\infty$ and the constant order parameter $r_\infty$ are determined by a self-consistency relation,
L000131	first derived in~\cite{SakK1986} and later formulated in a more convenient complex form in~\cite{OmeW2012},
L000132	\begin{equation}
L000133	\fr{1}{K} e^{i \lambda} = i \int_{-\infty}^\infty g(\Omega_\infty + K r_\infty s) h(s) ds,
L000134	\label{Eq:SC}
L000135	\end{equation}
L000136	where
L000137	\begin{equation}
L000138	h(s) = \left\{
L000139	\begin{array}{lcl}
L000140	( 1 - \sqrt{1 - s^{-2}} ) s & \mbox{ for } & |s| > 1 \\[1mm]
L000141	s - i \sqrt{1-s^2} & \mbox{ for } & |s|\le 1.
L000142	\end{array}
L000143	\right.
L000144	\label{Def:h}
L000145	\end{equation}
L000146	
L000147	However, recently finite-size effects have gained much attention. Numerical experiments have led to a better understanding of finite-size effects and provided clear evidence for their significance~\cite{Dai1994,Dai1996,CraD1999,NisTHA2012,HonCTP2015,SnyderEtAl21,YueG2024,SumJ2024}. In particular, several finite-size phenomena were observed such as a stochastic drift of oscillators which disappears in the thermodynamic limit \cite{GiacominPoquet15,Lucon15,Got2015,Gottwald17} and emergent random chimera switching in a deterministic two-population KS model \cite{IrvG2025}. Capturing finite-size effects analytically is a notoriously hard problem. Relaxation rates and pair correlation functions were obtained using kinetic theory~\cite{HildebrandEtAl07,BuiceChow07,YoonEtAl15} and the finite-size scaling near criticality has been successfully quantified~\cite{HongEtAl07,HonCTP2015,HonOS2016,ParkPark24}.
L000148	Recently, substantial progress has been made
L000149	for stochastic systems of coupled oscillators \cite{TyuGKP2018,Bue2025}.
L000150	However, analytical methods for deterministic
L000151	systems still remain underdeveloped.
L000152	A general scheme for analyzing fluctuations in deterministic systems
L000153	of coupled phase oscillators was proposed in~\cite{KurN1987,Daido89,Dai1990}.
L000154	In particular, accurate approximations were obtained in the subcritical case of the KS model~\eqref{Eq:KS:original} with $\lambda=0$
L000155	and a Lorentzian frequency distribution~\cite{Dai1990}.
L000156	However, this approach relies on several approximations
L000157	which do not carry over to the case of more general frequency distributions
L000158	such as Gaussians as we will show (see Appendix~\ref{app:daido}).
L000159	In this Letter we will capture finite-size effects in a first principle way
L000160	and provide explicit expressions for the covariance function
L000161	and the variance of the order parameter.
L000162	
L000163	Our theory is based on the assumption that fluctuations in the deterministic KS model constitute a Gaussian process decaying with $1/\sqrt{N}$ and determined entirely by its mean and covariance function (see Appendix~\ref{app:CLT}
L000164	for numerical evidence for this assumption).
L000165	This assumption would naturally be satisfied if their dynamics were governed
L000166	by an underlying functional central limit theorem.
L000167	We will additionally adopt several technical assumptions on replacing finite-size quantities
L000168	by their thermodynamic mean-field limits as well as replacing sums
L000169	by integrals which requires a sufficiently large system size $N$.
L000170	
L000171	Specifically, we consider the modified order parameter
L000172	$$
L000173	Z_\mathrm{mod}(t) = Z(t) \frac{\overline{Z_\mathrm{c}(t)}}{|Z_\mathrm{c}(t)|}
L000174	\quad\mbox{with}\quad
L000175	Z_\mathrm{c}(t) = \frac{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j\in\mathcal{C}} e^{i \psi_j(t)},
L000176	$$
L000177	where the bar denotes the complex-conjugate. Compared to the original order parameter \eqref{Def:Z}, the modified order parameter is represented in the frame of reference moving with the synchronized cluster. This ensures that, contrary to $Z(t)$, the modified order parameter does not exhibit phase diffusion~\cite{PikR1999,PikBI2026} (see Appendix~\ref{app:phasediff}).
L000178	Recently, it was numerically recognized that fluctuations
L000179	\begin{equation}
L000180	\zeta(t) = \sqrt{N} ( Z_\mathrm{mod}(t) - \langle Z_\mathrm{mod}(t) \rangle )
L000181	\label{Def:zeta}
L000182	\end{equation}
L000183	around the thermodynamic mean $\langle Z_\mathrm{mod}(t) \rangle$,
L000184	where $\langle\cdot\rangle$ denotes time averaging, can be approximated by a Gaussian process \cite{YueG2024,IrvG2025}.
L000185	Fluctuations in $Z_\mathrm{mod}(t)$,
L000186	with a variance that decays as $1/N$ and vanishes in the thermodynamic limit,
L000187	lead to fluctuations in other macroscopic variables,
L000188	including the order parameter with fluctuations
L000189	\begin{equation}
L000190	\delta(t) = \sqrt{N} ( |Z_\mathrm{mod}(t)| - \langle |Z_\mathrm{mod}(t)| \rangle ) = \sqrt{N} ( r(t) - \langle r(t) \rangle ).
L000191	\label{Def:delta}
L000192	\end{equation}
L000193	Gaussian processes are entirely determined by their mean, accessible via classical mean-field theory, and their covariance function
L000194	\begin{equation}
L000195	R(\tau) = \langle \zeta(t) \overline{\zeta(t+\tau)} \rangle.
L000196	\label{Def:R}
L000197	\end{equation}
L000198	Previous work~\cite{YueG2024,IrvG2025} has shown the efficacy of such an approach, however, these works relied on approximating the general Gaussian process by an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process the parameters of which were estimated only numerically and needed to be recalculated for each set of equation parameters. Moreover, a precise a priori knowledge of which oscillators are synchronized and which are not was required.
L000199	
L000200	In this Letter, we find explicit analytical expressions for the covariance function \eqref{Def:R} and the variance of finite-size fluctuations of the order parameter $V = \langle \delta^2(t) \rangle$, given entirely in terms of equation parameters $K$, $\lambda$ and $g(\omega)$. We present expressions for both the partially synchronized and the completely incoherent state. Remarkably, our expressions do not require any a priori knowledge about which oscillators partake in the synchronized cluster and which do not.
L000201	This opens up the way to perform stochastic model reduction
L000202	for deterministic systems of coupled oscillators
L000203	where the drift and diffusion coefficients are given entirely
L000204	in terms of the microscopic equation parameters.
L000205	Such reductions have so far been restricted to multi-scale systems \cite{Givonetal04}.
L000206	
L000207	Our main results can be formulated as follows. For partially synchronized states we predict
L000208	\begin{equation}
L000209	R_\mathrm{ps}(\tau) = K r_\infty \int\limits_{-\infty}^\infty \fr{(\overline{h^2(s)} - 1) ( 1 - |h^2(s)| ) g(\Omega_\infty + K r_\infty s) }{\overline{h^2(s)} - \exp(i K r_\infty s \sqrt{1 - s^{-2}}\:\tau)} ds
L000210	\label{Formula:R:ps}
L000211	\end{equation}
L000212	and
L000213	\begin{equation}
L000214	V_\mathrm{ps} = \fr{1}{2} R_\mathrm{ps}(0) + \mathcal{O}\left(\fr{1}{\sqrt{N}}\right)
L000215	\quad\mbox{for}\quad N\gg 1.
L000216	\label{Formula:V:ps}
L000217	\end{equation}
L000218	For completely incoherent states we find
L000219	\begin{equation}
L000220	R_\mathrm{incoh}(\tau) = \int_{-\infty}^\infty g(\omega) e^{-i\omega\tau} d\omega
L000221	\label{Formula:R:incoh}
L000222	\end{equation}
L000223	and
L000224	\begin{equation}
L000225	\sqrt{N} \langle r(t) \rangle = \fr{\sqrt{\pi}}{2}\quad\mbox{and}\quad
L000226	V_\mathrm{incoh} = 1 - \fr{\pi}{4}\quad\mbox{for}\quad N\gg 1.
L000227	\label{Formula:incoh:r}
L000228	\end{equation}
L000229	Our analytical results and their efficacy in capturing finite-size fluctuations
L000230	are illustrated in Figs.~\ref{Fig:Covariances} and~\ref{Fig:OP:OPVariance},
L000231	where we compare our predictions with simulations of the full KS model \eqref{Eq:KS:original} for a network of $1,000$ oscillators
L000232	with a Gaussian natural frequency distribution $g(\omega)$ with unit-variance. Fig.~\ref{Fig:Covariances} shows the covariance function $R(\tau)$ for a partially synchronized state described by \eqref{Formula:R:ps}
L000233	and for a completely incoherent state described by \eqref{Formula:R:incoh}. Fig.~\ref{Fig:OP:OPVariance}(a) shows the averaged order parameter $\langle r(t) \rangle$ as a function of the coupling strength $K$. The order parameter of the full KS model \eqref{Eq:KS:original} is very well reproduced by classical mean-field theory \eqref{Eq:SC} for values of the coupling strength $K\ge K_{\rm{crit}}$. Moreover, our extension of the mean-field results to the completely incoherent state given by \eqref{Formula:incoh:r} reproduces the order parameter for the subcritical range $K<K_{\rm{crit}}$. Fig.~\ref{Fig:OP:OPVariance}(b) shows that the variance of the order parameter is well captured by our analytical expressions~\eqref{Formula:V:ps} and \eqref{Formula:incoh:r} for coupling strength away from the bifurcation at $K_{\rm{crit}}$.
L000234	Additional numerical results and the description
L000235	of our numerical setup are provided
L000236	in Appendix~\ref{app:addnum}
L000237	and Appendix~\ref{app:num}, respectively.
L000238	
L000239	\begin{figure}
L000240	\begin{center}
L000241	\includegraphics[height=0.48\columnwidth,angle=270]{ReR_K6.pdf}\hspace{0.02\columnwidth}
L000242	\includegraphics[height=0.48\columnwidth,angle=270]{ImR_K6.pdf}\\[1mm]
L000243	\includegraphics[height=0.48\columnwidth,angle=270]{ReR_K0_5.pdf}\hspace{0.02\columnwidth}
L000244	\includegraphics[height=0.48\columnwidth,angle=270]{ImR_K0_5.pdf}
L000245	\end{center}
L000246	\caption{Covariance $R(\tau)$ of fluctuations $\zeta(t)$ of the complex order parameter for the KS model \eqref{Eq:KS:original}. (Top row) Partially synchronized state at $K = 6$ and $\lambda = \pi/4$, with theoretical prediction \eqref{Formula:R:ps}. (Bottom row) Completely incoherent state at $K = 0.5$ and $\lambda = \pi/4$, with theoretical prediction \eqref{Formula:R:incoh}.}
L000247	\label{Fig:Covariances}
L000248	\end{figure}
L000249	
L000250	\begin{figure}
L000251	\begin{center}
L000252	\includegraphics[height=0.48\columnwidth,angle=270]{OP.pdf}\hspace{0.02\columnwidth}
L000253	\includegraphics[height=0.48\columnwidth,angle=270]{OPVariance.pdf}
L000254	\end{center}
L000255	\caption{(a) Averaged order parameter $\langle r(t) \rangle$, with theoretical predictions from classical mean-field theory \eqref{Eq:SC} for $K>K_{\rm{crit}}\approx 1.92$ and from our approach \eqref{Formula:incoh:r} for $K<K_{\rm{crit}}$, and (b) its variance $V$,
L000256	with theoretical predictions~\eqref{Formula:V:ps}, \eqref{Formula:incoh:r},
L000257	versus coupling strength $K$ in the KS model~\eqref{Eq:KS:original} with $\lambda = \pi/4$.}
L000258	\label{Fig:OP:OPVariance}
L000259	\end{figure}
L000260	
L000261	
L000262	
L000263	
L000264	{\it Covariance function for a partially synchronized state.}
L000265	Our approach is based on the following prerequisites.
L000266	(i) Moving into the corotating frame
L000267	$\theta_i(t) = \psi_i(t) - \arg Z_\mathrm{c}(t)$ and assuming that the rotation frequency and the order parameter are given to leading order by their respective mean-field limits, Eq.~(\ref{Eq:KS:original}) becomes the so called Adler equation
L000268	\begin{equation}
L000269	\dot{\theta}_i = \omega_i - \Omega_\infty + K\: \mathrm{Im}\left( Z_\infty e^{-i \theta_i} e^{-i \lambda} \right).
L000270	\label{eq:Adler0}
L000271	\end{equation}
L000272	
L000273	(ii)~We use the well-known fact (see Appendix~\ref{app:MFT} for details)
L000274	that the probability density of the solution of the Adler equation \eqref{eq:Adler0}
L000275	is given by the Ott-Antonson ansatz~\cite{OttA2008}
L000276	\begin{equation}
L000277	f_\phi(\theta,\omega) = \fr{g(\omega)}{2\pi} \left\{ 1 + \sum\limits_{n=1}^\infty
L000278	\left[ \overline{z}_\phi^n(\omega) e^{i n \theta} + z_\phi^n(\omega) e^{-i n \theta} \right] \right\},
L000279	\label{Def:f_phi}
L000280	\end{equation}
L000281	where
L000282	\begin{equation}
L000283	z_\phi(\omega) = e^{i \phi} h\left( \fr{\omega - \Omega_\infty}{K r_\infty} \right).
L000284	\label{Formula:zphi}
L000285	\end{equation}
L000286	It follows directly from (\ref{Def:f_phi}) that for every non-negative integer~$n$ we have
L000287	\begin{equation}
L000288	\int_0^{2\pi} \fr{f_\phi(\theta,\omega)}{g(\omega)} e^{i n \theta} d\theta = z_\phi^n(\omega),
L000289	\label{Formula:OA}
L000290	\end{equation}
L000291	which, upon employing (\ref{Def:Z}) and~(\ref{Eq:SC}), implies
L000292	\begin{equation}
L000293	Z_\infty = \int_{-\infty}^\infty d\omega \int_0^{2\pi} f_\phi(\theta,\omega) e^{i \theta} d\theta
L000294	= - r_\infty i e^{i \lambda} e^{i \phi}.
L000295	\label{Z_mod:infty}
L000296	\end{equation}
L000297	Moreover, if $\theta_i(t)$ is a solution of the scalar Adler equation~(\ref{eq:Adler0}),
L000298	then for any $2\pi$-periodic function $F(\theta)$ we have the ergodicity property
L000299	\begin{equation}
L000300	\langle F(\theta_j(t)) \rangle = \int_0^{2\pi} \fr{f_\phi(\theta,\omega_j)}{g(\omega_j)} F(\theta) d\theta.
L000301	\label{Ergodicity}
L000302	\end{equation}
L000303	(iii)~Finally, we establish an explicit solution of the Adler equation \eqref{eq:Adler0}.
L000304	Introducing $\Delta_j = \omega_j - \Omega_\infty$ and $H_\infty = K Z_\infty e^{-i \lambda}$,
L000305	solutions of the Adler equation $\theta_j(t)$ with initial conditions $\theta_j(0)$
L000306	are given for $|\Delta_j| \ne |H_\infty|$ by
L000307	\begin{equation}
L000308	e^{i \theta_j(t)} = W( t, H_\infty,\Delta_j, e^{i \theta_j(0)} ),
L000309	\label{Formula:SolAdler}
L000310	\end{equation}
L000311	with
L000312	\begin{equation}
L000313	W( t, H, \Delta, \sigma ) = H a_1(t) + \fr{a_2( t) \sigma}{1 + \overline{H} a_1( t) \sigma},
L000314	\label{MainSln}
L000315	\end{equation}
L000316	where
L000317	$$
L000318	a_1(t) = i (e^{i \xi t} - 1) / \chi_1(t)
L000319	\quad\mbox{and}\quad
L000320	a_2(t) = 4 \xi^2 e^{i \xi t} / \chi_1^2(t)
L000321	$$
L000322	with $\chi_1( t ) = (\Delta - \xi) e^{i \xi t} - (\Delta + \xi)$ and
L000323	\begin{equation}
L000324	\xi = \left\{
L000325	\begin{array}{lcl}
L000326	- i \sqrt{ |H|^2 - \Delta^2 } & \mbox{ for } & |\Delta| \le |H|,\\[2mm]
L000327	\hphantom{-i} \sqrt{ \Delta^2 - |H|^2 } & \mbox{ for } & |\Delta| > |H|.
L000328	\end{array}
L000329	\right.
L000330	\label{Def:xi}
L000331	\end{equation}
L000332	Note that for $|\Delta_j| < |H_\infty|$, the $j$th oscillator is coherent
L000333	and the dynamics of $\theta_j(t)$ converges to a fixed point as $t\to\infty$,
L000334	while for $|\Delta_j| > |H_\infty|$, the oscillator is incoherent with underlying periodic dynamics.
L000335	Details on the derivation of~\eqref{Formula:SolAdler}, \eqref{MainSln} are provided in Appendix~\ref{app:Adler}.
L000336	
L000337	The covariance function (\ref{Def:R}) can be expressed in terms of the fluctuations (\ref{Def:zeta}) as
L000338	\begin{eqnarray}
L000339	R_\mathrm{ps}(\tau) &=&  N \langle
L000340	Z_\mathrm{mod}(t) \overline{Z_\mathrm{mod}(t+\tau)}
L000341	\rangle - N |Z_\infty|^2
L000342	\nonumber\\
L000343	&=&
L000344	\fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j,k=1}^N \langle e^{i \theta_j(t)} e^{-i \theta_k(t+\tau)} \rangle - N |Z_\infty|^2.
L000345	\label{Formula:U:1}
L000346	\end{eqnarray}
L000347	If either $j\in\mathcal{C}$ or $k\in\mathcal{C}$ the phase asymptotically approaches a fixed point and the summands can be written as
L000348	\begin{equation}
L000349	\langle e^{i \theta_j(t)}e^{-i \theta_k(t+\tau)} \rangle = \langle e^{i \theta_j(t)} \rangle \langle e^{-i \theta_k(t)} \rangle.
L000350	\label{IndividualCorrealtions:theta}
L000351	\end{equation}
L000352	Moreover, the independence identity~\eqref{IndividualCorrealtions:theta} also holds
L000353	if $j,k\in\mathcal{R}$ and $\sqrt{\Delta_j^2 - |H_\infty|^2}$
L000354	and $\sqrt{\Delta_k^2 - |H_\infty|^2}$ are incommensurable (see Appendix~\ref{app:indep}),
L000355	and hence generically the sum in~\eqref{Formula:U:1} can be written as
L000356	\begin{eqnarray*}
L000357	&&
L000358	\sum\limits_{j,k=1}^N \langle e^{i \theta_j(t)} e^{-i \theta_k(t+\tau)} \rangle
L000359	= \sum\limits_{j,k=1}^N \langle e^{i \theta_j(t)} \rangle \langle e^{-i \theta_k(t)} \rangle \\
L000360	&&
L000361	+ \sum\limits_{j\in\mathcal{R}} \langle e^{i \theta_j(t)} e^{-i \theta_j(t+\tau)} \rangle
L000362	- \sum\limits_{j\in\mathcal{R}} \left| \langle e^{i \theta_j(t)} \rangle \right|^2.
L000363	\end{eqnarray*}
L000364	Substitution into \eqref{Formula:U:1} yields,
L000365	upon using $\langle Z_\mathrm{mod}(t)\rangle = Z_\infty$,
L000366	\begin{equation}
L000367	    R_\mathrm{ps}(\tau) =
L000368	\frac{1}{N}\sum\limits_{j\in\mathcal{R}} \langle e^{i \theta_j(t)} e^{-i \theta_j(t+\tau)} \rangle
L000369	- \frac{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j\in\mathcal{R}} \left| \langle e^{i \theta_j(t)} \rangle \right|^2.
L000370	\label{Formula:R:ps2}
L000371	\end{equation}
L000372	Now, using~\eqref{Ergodicity} to replace temporal averages by averages over the phases and the explicit formula~\eqref{Formula:SolAdler} for $e^{i \theta_j(t+\tau)}$, we obtain
L000373	\begin{eqnarray*}
L000374	\langle e^{i \theta_j(t)} \rangle
L000375	&=& z_\phi(\omega_j),\\[1mm]
L000376	\left\langle e^{i \theta_j(t)} e^{-i \theta_j(t+\tau)} \right\rangle
L000377	&=&
L000378	\int_0^{2\pi} e^{i \theta} \overline{W}( \tau, H_\infty,\Delta_j, e^{i \theta} ) \times \\
L000379	\times\fr{f_\phi(\theta,\omega_j)}{g(\omega_j)} d\theta
L000380	&=& \fr{\overline{H a_1(\tau)}}{\overline{z_\phi(\omega_j)}} ( |z_\phi(\omega_j)|^2 - 1 )
L000381	+ 1.
L000382	\end{eqnarray*}
L000383	(Note that for the second correlation, we used identity~\eqref{Formula:OA}
L000384	after expanding the solution \eqref{Formula:SolAdler}--\eqref{MainSln} in a geometric series.
L000385	Details of the calculations can be found in Appendix~\ref{app:Adler}.)
L000386	Altogether, this yields
L000387	$$
L000388	R_\mathrm{ps}(\tau) =
L000389	\fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N
L000390	\left[ \fr{\overline{H a_1(\tau)}}{\overline{z_\phi(\omega_j)}}
L000391	- 1 \right] \left( | z_\phi(\omega_j) |^2 - 1 \right).
L000392	$$
L000393	Finally, to obtain a compact expression, we make the technical assumption that $N\gg 1$,
L000394	and replace  averaging over indices $j$
L000395	by averaging over the distribution $g(\omega)$, and write
L000396	\begin{equation}
L000397	R_\mathrm{ps}(\tau) = \int\limits_{-\infty}^\infty
L000398	\left[ \fr{\overline{H a_1(\tau)}}{\overline{z_\phi(\omega)}}
L000399	- 1 \right] \left( | z_\phi(\omega) |^2 - 1 \right) g(\omega) d\omega.
L000400	\label{Formula:U1}
L000401	\end{equation}
L000402	After some algebraic manipulations (see Appendix~\ref{app:Derivation}), the covariance function~\eqref{Formula:U1} simplifies to our expression~\eqref{Formula:R:ps}. Note that if $|\omega - \Omega_\infty|\le |H_\infty| = K r_\infty$, $|z_\phi| = 1$. Therefore such a set of $\omega$ does not contribute to the integral $R_\mathrm{ps}(\tau)$. This has the desirable consequence that in order to evaluate the covariance function, no prior knowledge is required about which oscillators are and which are not synchronized.
L000403	
L000404	
L000405	
L000406	
L000407	
L000408	
L000409	{\it Covariance function for a completely incoherent state.}
L000410	Our analysis of the completely incoherent state is based on the following prerequisites.
L000411	(i) In this state, the phases $\psi_j$ tend to be uniformly distributed on the interval $[0,2\pi]$,
L000412	and their probability density in the thermodynamic limit is given by
L000413	\begin{equation}
L000414	f_\mathrm{incoh}(\psi,\omega,t) = \fr{g(\omega)}{2\pi}.
L000415	\label{Def:f_incoh}
L000416	\end{equation}
L000417	(ii) The global order parameter shows no collective oscillations, and $Z(t) = 0$ as $N\to\infty$. This implies that the dynamics of each oscillator is approximately described by $\dot{\psi}_i = \omega_i$,
L000418	and therefore $\psi_i(t) = \omega_i t + \psi_i(0)$.
L000419	
L000420	Consider the fluctuations
L000421	$$
L000422	\zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t) = \sqrt{N}( Z(t) - \langle Z(t) \rangle)
L000423	$$
L000424	of the complex order parameter in this incoherent state.
L000425	The corresponding covariance function is then given by
L000426	\begin{eqnarray*}
L000427	R_\mathrm{incoh}(\tau) &=& \langle \zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t) \overline{\zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t+\tau)} \rangle \\
L000428	&=& \fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j,k = 1}^N \left\langle e^{i \psi_j(t)}e^{-i \psi_k(t+\tau)} \right\rangle - N | \langle Z(t) \rangle |^2.
L000429	\end{eqnarray*}
L000430	For different incommensurable frequencies $\omega_j$ and $\omega_k$,
L000431	the variables $e^{i \psi_j(t)}$ and $e^{i \psi_k(t)}$ are independent,
L000432	which implies again an identity of the form \eqref{IndividualCorrealtions:theta}, and we obtain
L000433	$$
L000434	R_\mathrm{incoh}(\tau) = \fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j = 1}^N \left\langle e^{i \psi_j(t)} e^{-i \psi_j(t+\tau)} \right\rangle
L000435	- \fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j = 1}^N \left| \langle e^{i \psi_j(t)} \rangle \right|^2.
L000436	$$
L000437	Moreover, using the ergodicity property and the stationary density for incoherent oscillators \eqref{Def:f_incoh}, we find
L000438	\begin{equation}
L000439	\langle e^{i \psi_j(t)} \rangle = \int_0^{2\pi} e^{i \psi} \fr{f_\mathrm{incoh}(\psi,\omega_j,t)}{g(\omega_j)} d\psi = 0
L000440	\label{Formula:Incoh}
L000441	\end{equation}
L000442	and
L000443	$$
L000444	\langle e^{i \psi_j(t)} e^{-i \psi_j(t+\tau)} \rangle = e^{- i \omega_j \tau}.
L000445	$$
L000446	Finally, in the large-$N$ limit we can replace averaging over indices $j$
L000447	by averaging over the distribution $g(\omega)$
L000448	and arrive at our expression~\eqref{Formula:R:incoh}
L000449	for the covariance function $R_{\rm{incoh}}(\tau)$.
L000450	Note that due to the normalization condition for $g(\omega)$, we have
L000451	$\langle \left| \zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t) \right|^2 \rangle = R_\mathrm{incoh}(0) = 1$.
L000452	
L000453	
L000454	
L000455	
L000456	{\it Variance of the order parameter for a partially synchronized state.}
L000457	Rewriting \eqref{Def:zeta} as
L000458	$$
L000459	Z_\mathrm{mod}(t)
L000460	= \langle Z_\mathrm{mod}(t) \rangle + \fr{1}{\sqrt{N}} \zeta(t) = Z_\infty + \fr{1}{\sqrt{N}} \zeta(t)
L000461	$$
L000462	implies
L000463	$$
L000464	|Z_\mathrm{mod}(t)|^2 = | Z_\infty |^2 + \fr{2}{\sqrt{N}} \Real\left( \overline{Z_\infty} \zeta(t) \right) + \fr{1}{N} |\zeta(t)|^2,
L000465	$$
L000466	which upon expansion for $N\gg 1$ becomes
L000467	$$
L000468	|Z_\mathrm{mod}(t)| = | Z_\infty | + \fr{1}{\sqrt{N}| Z_\infty |} \Real\left( \overline{Z_\infty} \zeta(t) \right) + \mathcal{O}\left(\fr{1}{N}\right).
L000469	$$
L000470	This allows to express the fluctuations of the order parameter \eqref{Def:delta} as
L000471	$$
L000472	\delta(t) = \fr{1}{| Z_\infty |} \Real\left( \overline{Z_\infty} \zeta(t) \right) + \mathcal{O}\left( \fr{1}{\sqrt{N}} \right),
L000473	$$
L000474	and the variance of the order parameter becomes
L000475	$$
L000476	V_\mathrm{ps}
L000477	= \fr{1}{2}
L000478	\Real\left( \fr{\overline{Z_\infty^2}}{| Z_\infty |^2} \tilde{R}_\mathrm{ps}(0) \right) + \fr{1}{2} R_\mathrm{ps}(0) + \mathcal{O}\left( \fr{1}{\sqrt{N}} \right),
L000479	$$
L000480	where $\tilde R_{\rm{ps}}(t)$ denotes
L000481	the pseudo-covariance
L000482	$\tilde R_{\rm{ps}}(t) = \langle \zeta(t) \zeta(t+\tau) \rangle$.
L000483	Employing calculations similar to those
L000484	for $R_\mathrm{ps}(t)$ (see Appendix~\ref{app:Pseudocov} for details),
L000485	we can show that $\tilde{R}_\mathrm{ps}(\tau) = 0$ for all $\tau\ge 0$.
L000486	The vanishing of the pseudo-covariance can be intuitively understood by evoking a symmetry between the real and imaginary parts of the fluctuations $\zeta$ and their independence. Using  $\tilde{R}_\mathrm{ps}(\tau) = 0$ for all $\tau\ge 0$, leads to our result \eqref{Formula:V:ps}.
L000487	
L000488	
L000489	
L000490	
L000491	{\it Variance of the order parameter for a completely incoherent state.}
L000492	In the large-$N$ limit, for a completely incoherent state
L000493	we have $\langle Z(t) \rangle = 0$, and therefore
L000494	$
L000495	|Z(t)| = |\zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t)| / \sqrt{N}.
L000496	$
L000497	Using the definition of $\zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t)$, we write
L000498	$$
L000499	\zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t) = \fr{1}{\sqrt{N}} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N \cos \psi_j(t) + \fr{i}{\sqrt{N}} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N \sin\psi_j(t).
L000500	$$
L000501	
L000502	
L000503	
L000504	
L000505	In the completely incoherent state all phases $\psi_j(t)$ are uniformly distributed in $[0,2\pi]$. This implies that in the large-$N$ limit fluctuations satisfy a central limit theorem, and $\zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t)$ is a two-dimensional random vector
L000506	distributed according to a bivariate normal distribution. The mean and variances are readily determined. For each $t\ge 0$ the function $\cos \psi_j(t)$ is a random variable with zero expected value $\langle \cos\psi_j \rangle_\mathrm{ens} = 0$ and variance
L000507	$$
L000508	\left\langle \cos^2\psi_j \right\rangle_\mathrm{ens} =
L000509	\fr{1}{2\pi} \int_0^{2\pi} \cos^2 \psi\: d\psi = \fr{1}{2},
L000510	$$
L000511	where $\langle \cdot \rangle_\mathrm{ens}$ denotes the ensemble average. Similarly, $\sin\psi_j(t)\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1/2)$. Moreover, the random variables $\cos\psi_j(t)$ and $\sin\psi_j(t)$ are uncorrelated since
L000512	$$
L000513	\langle \cos\psi_j \sin\psi_j \rangle_\mathrm{ens}
L000514	= \fr{1}{2\pi} \int_0^{2\pi} \cos\psi \sin\psi\:d\psi = 0.
L000515	$$
L000516	Hence $\Real(\zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t))\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1/2)$ and $\Imag(\zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t))\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1/2)$, and $\left| \zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t) \right|$ has a Rayleigh distribution with expected value
L000517	$$
L000518	\langle \left| \zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t) \right| \rangle = \sqrt{\pi}/2
L000519	$$
L000520	and variance
L000521	$$
L000522	V_\mathrm{incoh} = \langle \left| \zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t) \right|^2 \rangle - \langle \left| \zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t) \right| \rangle^2 = 1 - \pi/4.
L000523	$$
L000524	Note that the value $\langle \left| \zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t) \right|^2 \rangle = 1$, which follows from the last two identities, agrees with the value $\langle \left| \zeta_\mathrm{incoh}(t) \right|^2 \rangle = R_\mathrm{incoh}(0)$ obtained above.
L000525	
L000526	
L000527	
L000528	
L000529	{\it Discussion and outlook.}
L000530	In this Letter, we have proposed a fully analytical approach
L000531	to capture the statistical behavior of finite-size fluctuations
L000532	in the deterministic KS model~\eqref{Eq:KS:original}.
L000533	In particular, our approach provides
L000534	
L000535	(i) expressions for the covariance function of fluctuations of the complex order parameter
L000536	and the variance of the order parameter entirely in terms of the KS model parameters,
L000537	
L000538	(ii) expressions for both sub- and super-critical coupling strengths,
L000539	
L000540	(iii) expressions that do not require prior knowledge
L000541	about which oscillators are synchronized and which are not.
L000542	
L000543	Our theory relies on two main assumptions:
L000544	(a) finite-size fluctuations of the deterministic KS model~\eqref{Eq:KS:original}
L000545	are governed by a Gaussian process and (b) the phase dynamics can be approximated
L000546	by the Adler equation~\eqref{eq:Adler0}, where we neglect fluctuations
L000547	and approximate the mean values of the rotation frequency
L000548	and the order parameter by their mean-field limits.
L000549	Remarkably, the statistical independence of fluctuations of distinct oscillators,
L000550	which were essential for finding explicit expressions,
L000551	follows directly from our solution of the Adler equation.
L000552	The assumption of Gaussian statistics of finite-size fluctuations would naturally follow,
L000553	if one could show that the KS model supports an underlying functional central limit theorem.
L000554	This would in turn require large system sizes $N$ -- an assumption
L000555	we had already employed in~\eqref{Formula:U1} when replacing sums by integrals.
L000556	
L000557	Simulations for the full KS model showed
L000558	that our theory performs well away from criticality.
L000559	On the other hand, our approximations were less accurate
L000560	when approaching the critical coupling strength $K_{\rm{crit}}$,
L000561	demarcating the transition from a desynchronized to a partially synchronized state.
L000562	The explicit expressions for the covariance function rely
L000563	on the explicit solution of the Adler equation.
L000564	Hence, the quality of our theory crucially depends on the Adler equation
L000565	being a good approximation of the full system.
L000566	This is only guaranteed if fluctuations can be neglected in Eq.~(\ref{Eq:KS:original}),
L000567	and breaks down when approaching criticality
L000568	(see Appendix~\ref{app:lim} for details).
L000569	We envisage extensions of our framework to the near critical case
L000570	using a perturbative approach around the expressions derived in this work.
L000571	
L000572	Our approach is sufficiently general to allow for further applications
L000573	to other interacting particle systems such as $\theta$-neuron models
L000574	and more complex network topologies
L000575	going beyond the all-to-all coupling considered in this Letter.
L000576	The mathematical tools developed here can potentially be adapted
L000577	to study finite-size fluctuations in noisy coupled oscillator systems~\cite{MasKK2010,Luc2014,Gottwald17,TyuGKP2018,PikDG2019,DelgadinoEtAl21,ZagliEtAl23,Bue2025}
L000578	finite-size sampling effects \cite{PetP2018,PetGP2019,FialkowskiEtAl23}
L000579	and dynamics of complexified KS models~\cite{ThuSST2023,LeeBBSTT2024}.
L000580	Further, it is known that the incoherent state and partially synchronized states
L000581	in the KS model are weakly chaotic attractors,
L000582	where the chaoticity can be attributed to the presence of finite-size fluctuations.
L000583	Therefore, it is natural to expect that knowing the characteristics of finite-size fluctuations,
L000584	may aid in computing  finite-size corrections of Lyapunov exponents~\cite{CarGP2018}.
L000585	
L000586	In summary, our results fully specify the statistical behavior
L000587	of finite-size fluctuations in the KS model
L000588	which have been numerically shown to be Gaussian processes.
L000589	This opens up the way to perform systematic stochastic model reductions
L000590	for such interacting particle systems from first principles
L000591	and to construct reduced stochastic equations
L000592	for designated collective variables \cite{Givonetal04,SmithGottwald20}.
