L001288	\section{Phase diffusion}
L001289	\label{app:phasediff}
L001290	Mean-field theory for partially synchronized states
L001291	predicts that the argument of the complex order parameter
L001292	$$
L001293	Z(t) = \frac{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N e^{i \psi_j(t)},
L001294	$$
L001295	uniformly increases or decreases with time.
L001296	To subtract this drift, we can move into the corotating frame of reference and consider $\arg(Z(t)e^{-i\Omega t})$,
L001297	where $\Omega$ is the average speed. However, this quantity experiences non-stationary irregular behaviour,
L001298	resembling Brownian motion, as shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig:Diffusion}.
L001299	This phenomenon is known as {\it phase diffusion}~\cite{PikR1999,PikBI2026}.
L001300	Due to this phase diffusion, the function $Z(t)e^{-i \Omega t}$ is difficult to characterize.
L001301	Therefore, in our study we work with a modified order parameter
L001302	$$
L001303	Z_\mathrm{mod}(t) = Z(t) \frac{\overline{Z_\mathrm{c}(t)}}{|Z_\mathrm{c}(t)|},
L001304	$$
L001305	where
L001306	$$
L001307	Z_\mathrm{c}(t) = \frac{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j\in\mathcal{C}} e^{i \psi_j(t)}
L001308	$$
L001309	is the order parameter, defined as the sum over the coherent
L001310	(locked) oscillators only.
L001311	In numerical simulations,
L001312	we identify the $j$th oscillator as coherent
L001313	if its natural frequency $\omega_j$ satisfies
L001314	the locking condition
L001315	$|\omega_j - \Omega| \le K \langle |Z(t)| \rangle$.
L001316	It turns out, that the argument
L001317	of the modified order parameter $Z_\mathrm{mod}(t)$
L001318	does not exhibit phase diffusion phenomenon,
L001319	and the behavior of this complex function
L001320	is nicely approximated
L001321	by a two-dimensional Gaussian process~\cite{YueG2024}.
L001322	This numerical observation is crucial for our analytical approach.
L001323	
