L001492	\section{Daido's theory}
L001493	\label{app:daido}
L001494	In~\cite{Dai1990}, H. Daido proposed an analytical approach to approximate finite-size fluctuations
L001495	in the KS model~\eqref{Eq:KS:original} with $\lambda = 0$.
L001496	In particular, for the subcritical case he obtained explicit expressions
L001497	for the covariance function and the variance of the complex order parameter.
L001498	We follow here his approach and determine expressions for $\lambda\ne 0$
L001499	in the subcritical case, and show that unlike for a Lorentz distribution of the natural frequencies
L001500	this method is not well suited for a Gaussian frequency distribution.
L001501	
L001502	Let us assume that the dynamics of the $i$th oscillator can be represented as
L001503	\begin{equation}
L001504	\psi_i(t) = \Theta_i(t) + \fr{1}{\sqrt{N}} \vartheta_i(t),
L001505	\label{Ansatz:Daido}
L001506	\end{equation}
L001507	where $\Theta_i, \vartheta_i = \mathcal{O}(1)$ for $N\to\infty$.
L001508	Upon substitution into the unnumbered equation
L001509	below (\ref{Eq:KS:original}) in the main text,
L001510	which we recall for convenience here,
L001511	$$
L001512	\dot{\psi}_i = \omega_i + K\: \mathrm{Im}\left( Z(t) e^{-i \psi_i} e^{-i \lambda} \right),
L001513	$$
L001514	we obtain
L001515	$$
L001516	\dot{\Theta}_i + \fr{1}{\sqrt{N}} \dot{\vartheta}_i = \omega_i + K\: \mathrm{Im}\left( Z(t) e^{-i ( \Theta_i + \vartheta_i / \sqrt{N} )} e^{-i \lambda} \right).
L001517	$$
L001518	Next, assuming that $\langle Z(t)\rangle = 0$ and hence $Z(t) = \mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{N})$,
L001519	and equating separately the terms of order $\mathcal{O}(1)$
L001520	and the terms of order $\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{N})$, we obtain
L001521	\begin{equation}
L001522	\dot{\Theta}_i = \omega_i
L001523	\label{Eq:Theta_i}
L001524	\end{equation}
L001525	and
L001526	\begin{equation}
L001527	\fr{1}{\sqrt{N}} \dot{\vartheta}_i = K\: \mathrm{Im}\left( Z(t) e^{-i \Theta_i} e^{-i \lambda} \right).
L001528	\label{Eq:vartheta_i}
L001529	\end{equation}
L001530	Eq.~\eqref{Eq:Theta_i} is solved by $\Theta_i(t) = \omega_i t + \Theta_i(0)$.
L001531	Substituting this into Eq.~\eqref{Eq:vartheta_i}, we obtain
L001532	$$
L001533	\fr{1}{\sqrt{N}} \dot{\vartheta}_i(t) = K \int_0^t \mathrm{Im}\left( Z(t') e^{-i ( \omega_i t' + \Theta_i(0) )} e^{-i \lambda} \right) dt'
L001534	$$
L001535	with $\vartheta_i(0) = 0$.
L001536	Finally, substituting the expressions for the solution to Eqs.~\eqref{Eq:Theta_i}
L001537	and~\eqref{Eq:vartheta_i} into~\eqref{Ansatz:Daido}
L001538	and then substituting $\theta_i(t) = \psi_i(t)$
L001539	into the definition of the complex order parameter~\eqref{Def:Z},
L001540	we obtain a self-consistency equation for $Z(t)$:
L001541	\begin{eqnarray}
L001542	Z(t) &=& \fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N e^{i ( \omega_i t + \Theta_i(0) )} \times\\
L001543	&\times& \left( 1 + i K \int_0^t \mathrm{Im}\left( Z(t') e^{-i ( \omega_i t' + \Theta_i(0) )} e^{-i \lambda} \right) dt' \right) \nonumber\\
L001544	&=& \fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N e^{i ( \omega_j t + \Theta_j(0) )}\nonumber\\
L001545	&+& \fr{K}{2} e^{-i \lambda} \int_0^t Z(t') \fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N e^{i \omega_j (t - t')} dt' \nonumber\\
L001546	&-& \fr{K}{2} e^{i \lambda} \int_0^t \overline{Z}(t') \fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N e^{i ( \omega_j (t + t') + 2 \Theta_j(0) )} dt',
L001547	\label{Eq:Z:Daido}
L001548	\end{eqnarray}
L001549	where we employed a Taylor expansion of the exponential function for $|Z(t)|\ll 1$.
L001550	
L001551	In the next step, we will obtain an asymptotic formula for $Z(t)$ as $t\to\infty$.
L001552	For this, we first notice that the function
L001553	$$
L001554	\fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N e^{i ( \omega_j (t + t') + 2 \Theta_j(0) )}
L001555	$$
L001556	is vanishing as $t\to\infty$ and hence can be neglected.
L001557	Furthermore, for $N\gg 1$ we have
L001558	$$
L001559	\fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N e^{i \omega_j t} \mapsto \int_{-\infty}^\infty g(\omega) e^{i \omega t} d\omega
L001560	= \hat{g}(t).
L001561	$$
L001562	Thus, the complex order parameter $Z(t)$ given in~\eqref{Eq:Z:Daido}
L001563	is the solution of the following Volterra integral equation
L001564	\begin{eqnarray}
L001565	Z(t) &=& \fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N e^{i ( \omega_j t + \Theta_j(0) )} \nonumber\\
L001566	&+& \fr{K}{2} e^{-i \lambda} \int_0^t \hat{g}(t - t') Z(t') dt'.
L001567	\label{Eq:Daido}
L001568	\end{eqnarray}
L001569	This equation can be solved using the Laplace transformation
L001570	$$
L001571	\mathcal{L}\::\: f(t) \mapsto F(s) = \int_0^\infty e^{- s t} f(t) dt.
L001572	$$
L001573	In particular, due to the convolution theorem, Eq.~\eqref{Eq:Daido} we write
L001574	$$
L001575	(\mathcal{L}Z)(s) = \fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N \fr{e^{i \Theta_j(0)}}{s - i \omega_j} + \fr{K}{2} e^{-i \lambda} (\mathcal{L}\hat{g})(s) (\mathcal{L}Z)(s),
L001576	$$
L001577	and therefore obtain
L001578	$$
L001579	(\mathcal{L}Z)(s) = \fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N \left( 1 - \fr{K}{2} e^{-i \lambda} (\mathcal{L}\hat{g})(s) \right)^{-1} \fr{e^{i \Theta_j(0)}}{s - i \omega_j}.
L001580	$$
L001581	Now, assuming that
L001582	$$
L001583	Q(s) := 1 - \fr{K}{2} e^{-i \lambda} (\mathcal{L}\hat{g})(s) \ne 0
L001584	\quad\mbox{for all}\quad \mathrm{Re}(s)\ge 0
L001585	$$
L001586	and that $1/Q(s)$ is a meromorphic function in the half-plane $\mathrm{Re}(s) < 0$ that satisfies
L001587	\begin{equation}
L001588	\lim\limits_{p\to\infty} \max\limits_{\pi/2\le |\phi| \le \pi} |1/Q( p e^{i \phi} )| = 0,
L001589	\label{Condition:Q}
L001590	\end{equation}
L001591	we apply the inverse Laplace transformation and find that the long-term asymptotics of $Z(t)$ is given by
L001592	$$
L001593	Z(t) = \fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N \fr{e^{i ( \omega_j t + \Theta_j(0) )}}{Q(i \omega_j)}.
L001594	$$
L001595	
L001596	The covariance function of the complex order parameter $Z(t)$ is now evaluated as
L001597	\begin{eqnarray*}
L001598	R_\mathrm{Daido}(\tau) &=& N \langle Z(t) \overline{Z(t+\tau)} \rangle \\
L001599	&=& \fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j,k=1}^N \fr{e^{-i ( \omega_k \tau + \Theta_k(0) - \Theta_j(0) )}}{Q(i \omega_j) \overline{Q(i \omega_k)}} \langle e^{i ( \omega_j - \omega_k ) t} \rangle \\
L001600	&=& \fr{1}{N} \sum\limits_{j=1}^N \fr{e^{-i \omega_j \tau}}{|Q(i \omega_j)|^2}
L001601	\approx \int_{-\infty}^\infty g(\omega) \fr{e^{-i \omega \tau}}{|Q(i \omega)|^2} d\omega,
L001602	\end{eqnarray*}
L001603	where we used $\langle e^{i ( \omega_j - \omega_k ) t} \rangle = \delta_{jk}$.
L001604	The variance of the order parameter $r(t) = |Z(t)|$ in the subcritical case is then given by
L001605	\begin{equation}
L001606	V_\mathrm{Daido} = N \langle |Z(t)|^2 \rangle - N \langle |Z(t)| \rangle^2 = R_\mathrm{Daido}(0) - \fr{\pi}{4},
L001607	\label{Formula:V:Daido}
L001608	\end{equation}
L001609	where we used our result~\eqref{Formula:incoh:r} for $\langle |Z(t)| \rangle$.
L001610	\begin{figure}
L001611	\begin{center}
L001612	\includegraphics[height=0.6\columnwidth,angle=270]{OPVariance_Daido.pdf}
L001613	\end{center}
L001614	\caption{Variance $V$ of the order parameter $r(t)$ versus coupling strength $K$
L001615	in the subcritical case of the KS model~\eqref{Eq:KS:original}
L001616	with $\lambda = \pi/4$ and a Gaussian distribution.
L001617	Theoretical predictions from our approach~\eqref{Formula:incoh:r} (thick line)
L001618	and from Daido's theory~\eqref{Formula:V:Daido} (circles)
L001619	are compared with the results of numerical simulations
L001620	with $N=1,000$ phase oscillators (squares).
L001621	The vertical dashed line indicates the critical coupling strength.
L001622	}
L001623	\label{Fig:OPVariance_Daido}
L001624	\end{figure}
L001625	Although the above formulas have been shown to be in good agreement with the numerical observations obtained from the full KS model~\eqref{Eq:KS:original} with a Lorentzian frequency distribution and $\lambda = 0$,
L001626	in the case of a Gaussian frequency distribution they fail to reproduce the observed behavior as seen in Fig.~\ref{Fig:OPVariance_Daido}.
L001627	In particular, they can be worse than our $(K,\lambda)$-independent formulas~\eqref{Formula:R:incoh} and~\eqref{Formula:incoh:r}.
L001628	Note that condition~\eqref{Condition:Q} is not satisfied for a Gaussian frequency distribution.
L001629	
